Description: Drop a surface's pending explicit-sync commit when it is destroyed
 WlSurface::commit() with a linux-drm-syncobj acquire point adds an fd
 source to the Wayland event loop whose data is pending_context, but
 ~WlSurface() never removed it. When a client goes away (crash, or killed
 for a protocol error) while a commit waits on its fence, the fence later
 fires handle_buffer_ready on the freed surface: complete_commit() then
 walks a garbage std::list forever (endless std::advance), pinning the
 Wayland thread. The main loop blocks for good in WaylandConnector::stop()
 at the next session pause (VT switch), so the screen freezes.
Author: Umoja developers
Forwarded: not yet (bug report drafted in packaging/mir/upstream-bugs/)
Last-Update: 2026-11-09
---
--- a/src/server/frontend_wayland/wl_surface.cpp
+++ b/src/server/frontend_wayland/wl_surface.cpp
@@ -131,6 +131,25 @@ mf::WlSurface::WlSurface(
 
 mf::WlSurface::~WlSurface()
 {
+    /* A commit may still be waiting for its acquire point (explicit sync).
+     * The event source's data is pending_context, which dies with us: if the
+     * fence signals after the client has gone (it crashed, or was killed for
+     * a protocol error), handle_buffer_ready would run complete_commit() on
+     * freed memory. Seen as the Wayland thread spinning forever in
+     * std::advance over a garbage std::list, which in turn blocks the main
+     * loop in WaylandConnector::stop() at the next VT switch.
+     */
+    if (buffer_ready_source)
+    {
+        wl_event_source_remove(buffer_ready_source);
+        buffer_ready_source = nullptr;
+        if (pending_context)
+        {
+            pending_context->release.signal();
+            pending_context.reset();
+        }
+    }
+
     // We can't use a function try block as we want to access `client`:
     // "Before any catch clauses of a function-try-block on a destructor are entered,
     // all bases and non-variant members have already been destroyed."
